I'll go with a simpler, safer design: use the mutex to serialize path construction/creation of the same file between concurrent threads of one client (the threadpool has 4 threads). Actually open(O_CREAT) and mkdir are individually race-safe (EEXIST is fine), so the mutex isn't strictly necessary; but since the API provides one, we'll lock during directory creation to avoid interleaved mkdir/ENOENT weirdness on weird filesystems (the man page mentions fuse/ftpfs/davfs — network filesystems where racing mkdir+open may fail!). This is actually a good reason: on davfs/ftpfs, concurrent create+mkdir might not be atomic. So: in open_treefile, lock the mutex when creating directories/files. Reads (no O_CREAT) don't take the lock.

